│ To address the issue of incorrect indentation when using prepend   │
│ or append directives with the literalinclude directive in Sphinx,  │
│ we need to modify the logic in the LiteralInclude class to         │
│ preserve leading whitespace when applying these directives.        │
│                                                                    │
│ ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
│                            ✅ Reasoning                            │
│                                                                    │
│ The literalinclude directive in Sphinx currently strips leading    │
│ whitespace from the included content, which causes the indentation │
│ to be incorrect when using prepend or append directives. The       │
│ prepend and append methods are responsible for applying these      │
│ directives, and the current logic uses lstrip() or similar         │
│ operations that remove leading whitespace, leading to the observed │
│ indentation issue.                                                 │
│                                                                    │
│ The solution is to modify the prepend and append methods to        │
│ preserve the leading whitespace by not stripping it. Instead, the  │
│ prepend/append text should be inserted directly before the         │
│ included content, ensuring that the original whitespace is         │
│ preserved.                                                         │
│                                                                    │
│ ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
│                              🛠️ Patch                               │
│                                                                    │
│ [file]sphinx/ext/literalinclude/literalinclude.py[/file]           │
