╭─ Patch Generation ─────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ ┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓ │
│ ┃   Patch to Fix Incorrect Indentation with literalinclude and   ┃ │
│ ┃                         prepend/append                         ┃ │
│ ┗━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┛ │
│                                                                    │
│ The issue arises because the literalinclude directive in Sphinx    │
│ strips leading whitespace when using prepend or append options.    │
│ This causes the included XML to lose its indentation, leading to   │
│ poor formatting.                                                   │
│                                                                    │
│ To fix this, we need to ensure that the prepend and append options │
│ are applied to the included code while preserving the leading      │
│ whitespace. This requires modifying the literalinclude directive   │
│ processing logic to include the prepend and append strings before  │
│ the included code.                                                 │
│                                                                    │
│ ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
│                           Patch Details                            │
│                                                                    │
│ The fix involves modifying the literalinclude function in the      │
│ Sphinx extension to handle the prepend and append options          │
│ correctly. The original code strips the leading whitespace, but we │
